 Absolutely — yes! The **Elegoo IR remote + receiver** combo is perfect for adding remote control to your DC motor setup. It’s a great hands-on extension that ties together input, output, and real-time control. Here’s a breakdown:

---

## 🎮 Overview: Using an IR Remote to Control DC Motors

You’ll be using:
- An **IR receiver** module (usually KY-022 or similar)
- The **Elegoo IR remote** (hex keypad remote)
- An **Arduino** to decode IR signals and drive the motor through the **L9110 module**

---

## 🧰 What You’ll Need

| Component        | Use |
|------------------|-----|
| IR Receiver (e.g., KY-022) | Receives signal from remote |
| Elegoo IR Remote | Sends IR codes |
| Arduino Uno/Nano | Brain to interpret & control |
| L9110 Driver     | Motor interface |
| DC Motor         | Controlled output |
| Jumper wires     | Wiring |
| (Optional) Breadboard | For prototyping |

---

## 🔌 Wiring Summary

| IR Receiver Pin | Connect To Arduino |
|-----------------|--------------------|
| `OUT`           | Digital pin (e.g. 2)|
| `VCC`           | 5V                 |
| `GND`           | GND                |

| L9110 Input | Connect To Arduino |
|------------|--------------------|
| `A-IA`     | Digital pin (e.g. 9) |
| `A-IB`     | Digital pin (e.g. 10) |

---

## 📦 Install the IR Library

Install the **IRremote** library:
1. Open Arduino IDE
2. Go to `Tools > Manage Libraries...`
3. Search for `IRremote` by shirriff or z3t0
4. Install it

⚠️ As of version 3.x+, use `#include <IRremote.h>` and call `IrReceiver.begin(2);`

---

## 💻 Code Sketch: IR-Controlled Motor Direction

```cpp
#include <IRremote.h>

const int IR_PIN = 2;         // IR Receiver pin
const int A_IA = 9;           // Motor input 1
const int A_IB = 10;          // Motor input 2

void setup() {
  pinMode(A_IA, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(A_IB, OUTPUT);
  IrReceiver.begin(IR_PIN);
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  if (IrReceiver.decode()) {
    unsigned long code = IrReceiver.decodedIRData.command;
    Serial.print("IR Code: ");
    Serial.println(code, HEX); // Print in HEX

    switch (code) {
      case 0x18: // "2" button on Elegoo remote
        forward();
        break;
      case 0x52: // "8" button
        reverse();
        break;
      case 0x1C: // "OK" button
        stopMotor();
        break;
    }
    IrReceiver.resume(); // Prepare for next signal
  }
}

void forward() {
  digitalWrite(A_IA, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, LOW);
}

void reverse() {
  digitalWrite(A_IA, LOW);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, HIGH);
}

void stopMotor() {
  digitalWrite(A_IA, LOW);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, LOW);
}
```

---

## 🎯 Default Elegoo IR Codes (Selected)

| Button | Code (Hex) |
|--------|------------|
| "1"    | `0x45`     |
| "2"    | `0x18`     |
| "3"    | `0x7`      |
| "OK"   | `0x1C`     |
| "8"    | `0x52`     |

> You can run the sketch and open the Serial Monitor to see codes for any button.

