Want help turning this into a classroom handout or lab assignment format with diagrams and wiring? Or expand to 2-motor robot control with remote navigation (↑ ↓ ← →)?

Awesome — let’s go with the **L9110 motor driver**, a simple and compact H-bridge module great for controlling small DC motors from microcontrollers like Arduino.

---

## 🔌 L9110 Motor Driver: Overview

### 🧠 What It Is
The **L9110** is a dual-channel H-bridge driver:
- Controls **2 DC motors**
- Can drive each **forwards or backwards**
- Handles small current loads (~800 mA per channel peak)

Perfect for:
- Tiny robots
- Line-followers
- Projects with AA-battery-level power

---

## ⚙️ Pinout (typical L9110 module)

| Label   | Purpose                  |
|---------|--------------------------|
| A-IA    | Control pin for Motor A  |
| A-IB    | Control pin for Motor A  |
| B-IA    | Control pin for Motor B  |
| B-IB    | Control pin for Motor B  |
| VCC     | Motor power (2.5–12V)    |
| GND     | Ground                   |
| MA      | Motor A output (wire 1 & 2) |
| MB      | Motor B output (wire 1 & 2) |

> IA/IB are logic-level inputs from your Arduino.  
> MA/MB go to your motor wires.  
> VCC powers the motor itself (often shared with Arduino’s 5V if low current).

---

## 🔁 Motor Direction Logic

You control the motor using digital HIGH/LOW signals on IA and IB:

| IA     | IB     | Motor Direction   |
|--------|--------|-------------------|
| HIGH   | LOW    | Forward            |
| LOW    | HIGH   | Reverse            |
| LOW    | LOW    | Stop (coast)       |
| HIGH   | HIGH   | Brake (shorted)    |

---

## 🧪 Example Arduino Sketch (One Motor)

```cpp
// L9110 Motor Driver test (Motor A)
const int A_IA = 9;  // Arduino pin to L9110 A-IA
const int A_IB = 10; // Arduino pin to L9110 A-IB

void setup() {
  pinMode(A_IA, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(A_IB, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  // Forward
  digitalWrite(A_IA, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, LOW);
  delay(1000);

  // Stop
  digitalWrite(A_IA, LOW);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, LOW);
  delay(500);

  // Reverse
  digitalWrite(A_IA, LOW);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, HIGH);
  delay(1000);

  // Stop
  digitalWrite(A_IA, LOW);
  digitalWrite(A_IB, LOW);
  delay(1000);
}
```

---

## 🧪 Lab Idea: “Direction Control Test”

**Goal**: Use buttons or delay cycles to reverse the motor’s direction.

**Materials**:
- Arduino
- L9110 driver
- One small DC motor
- Jumper wires
- (Optional) Push buttons to toggle directions manually

---

## 🛠️ Gotchas

- Don’t connect high-current motors (>1A) — it’ll get hot or fail.
- Don’t power motors with >12V — the IC isn't rated for it.
- Always double-check wiring (no cross-polarity!)

